#!|
  A private function and a private constant, both declared and never used.

  This compiles. MANIFESTO.md lists what must be checked at compile time —
  exhaustive matching, linear consumption, borrow exclusivity, visibility,
  casing, *unused imports*, unhandled Result/Option, constant division by
  zero, type mismatches, effect purity — and an unused import is on that
  list where an unused declaration is not.

  The fixture exists to keep it that way. An unused-declaration rejection
  was written once and taken back out, because the only remedy it left was
  to mark demonstration code `pub` — which says an item is public API in
  order to stop the compiler complaining, exactly the local bandaid
  Milestone 6 forbids a diagnostic from steering anyone towards. It also
  had to be absorbed by the random-program generator, which is meant to
  emit valid programs and would otherwise have been emitting rejected ones.

  Reintroducing it is a language change: state it in MANIFESTO.md, and
  design a remedy that is not `pub`, before this fixture starts failing.
|#

fun unused_helper() I64
  return 1
end

const UNUSED_CONST: I64 = 5

pub fun main() I64
  return 0
end
